    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Hinduism has no single founder or creed: it grew from the Vedic religion of the north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and absorbed many local traditions over three thousand years.</li><li>Its central ideas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a>, karma, rebirth and release (moksha), and its devotion to gods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and the Goddess shaped the culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>.</li><li>Reformed and renewed in the 19th and 20th centuries, it is the world’s third-largest religion, with about 1.2 billion followers and communities on every continent.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, often called the largest religious monument in the world, was built in the early 12th century as a temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

