    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vedas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vedas.jpg" alt="Vedas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1500-1200_BCE_Rigveda,_manuscript_page_sample_i,_Mandala_1,_Hymn_1_(Sukta_1),_Adhyaya_1,_lines_1.1.1_to_1.1.9,_Sanskrit,_Devanagari.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ms Sarah Welch, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hinduism has no founder and no single moment of origin. It is the name given, from the outside and only in modern times, to a vast family of traditions that grew up in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> over more than three thousand years. The word “Hindu” comes from the Persian name for the people beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> river, the Sindhu; “Hinduism” as the name of a religion came into use in English in the early 19th century. Hindus themselves have often spoken of the sanatana <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a>, the “eternal order”.</p><p>Its oldest recorded root is the religion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, brought into the north-west of the subcontinent by speakers of Indo-Aryan languages in the second millennium BCE. The hymns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>, composed in an early form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> perhaps between 1500 and 1200 BCE, praise gods of nature and order such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a>, Agni and Varuna and accompany sacrifices performed by priests around a sacred fire. For centuries they were transmitted by memory alone, with remarkable precision.</p><p>Over the following centuries Vedic religion changed. Ritual specialists, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, developed elaborate sacrificial systems; then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a>, composed from about the 8th century BCE, turned inward, asking about the nature of the self and of ultimate reality and teaching that the two are one. At the same time movements of wandering ascetics, from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> also grew, challenged the authority of the Vedas and the value of sacrifice.</p><p>Between about 500 BCE and 500 CE the main features of classical Hinduism took shape. The great epics, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, told the stories of gods and heroes; law books defined dharma, the duties of each class and stage of life; and devotion to personal gods, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, grew alongside the older rites. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> of the 4th to 6th centuries, stone temples, sculpture and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a>, encyclopaedic collections of myth and ritual, gave this religion its classical form.</p><p>Hinduism also absorbed countless local traditions: village goddesses, the worship of trees, rivers and snakes, and the gods of peoples who came into the orbit of Sanskrit culture. Whether elements of it go back to the cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley civilisation</a>, as some scholars have suggested from its seals and figurines, remains uncertain.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vishnu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vishnu.jpg" alt="Vishnu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vishnu_from_Gita_Govinda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Manaku of Guler, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hindu traditions differ greatly in their gods, practices and philosophies, but most share a set of ideas. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a> is the order of the universe and the duty of each being within it. Karma is the law that actions bring their consequences, in this life or the next. Samsara is the cycle of birth, death and rebirth through which the soul passes according to its karma. Moksha is release from that cycle, the highest goal of life.</p><p>Classical texts describe four aims of human life: dharma, righteousness; artha, prosperity; kama, pleasure; and moksha, release. They also describe four stages of life for men of the higher classes: student, householder, forest dweller and renouncer. The paths to release are usually described as three: the way of action performed without attachment (karma yoga), the way of knowledge (jnana yoga) and the way of loving devotion to God (bhakti yoga), all of them taught in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>.</p><p>Most Hindus worship one or more personal gods, often understood as forms of a single ultimate reality. The major traditions are devoted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, who descends to earth in incarnations (avatars) such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>; to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, the ascetic and the lord of creation and destruction; and to the Goddess, Devi, in forms such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a>, Kali, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi">Lakshmi</a> and Parvati. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganesha">Ganesha</a>, the elephant-headed remover of obstacles, is honoured at the start of almost every undertaking.</p><p>Worship (puja) takes place at home shrines and in temples, where the image of the deity is treated as a living presence: bathed, dressed, offered food and lamps, and viewed by worshippers in the act of darshan, seeing and being seen by the god. Pilgrimage to sacred rivers and cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, and festivals such as Diwali, the festival of lights, and Holi, the festival of colours, mark the year. Rites of passage, the samskaras, accompany birth, initiation, marriage and death; the dead are usually cremated.</p><p>Philosophers built sophisticated systems on these foundations. The school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a>, based on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a>, divided between Advaita, which holds that the self and Brahman are identical and the world of distinctions an illusion, and schools that keep a real distinction between the soul and a personal God.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita.jpg" alt="Bhagavad Gita" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BhagavadGita-19th-century-Illustrated-Sanskrit-Chapter_1.20.21.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hindu scriptures are traditionally divided into two classes. Shruti, “what is heard”, is revealed knowledge received by the ancient seers: the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaveda">Samaveda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajurveda">Yajurveda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atharvaveda">Atharvaveda</a>, each with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanas">Brahmanas</a>, ritual commentaries; Aranyakas, forest treatises; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a>, philosophical dialogues. Smriti, “what is remembered”, is the tradition built upon them: the epics, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a>, the law books and many other works.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, at about 100,000 verses one of the longest poems in the world, tells of the war between two branches of a royal family. Within it stands the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>, a dialogue on the battlefield between the warrior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a> and his charioteer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, revealed as God, on duty, devotion and the nature of the self; it has become the best known of all Hindu texts. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, ascribed to the poet Valmiki, tells of the exile of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>, the abduction of his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sita">Sita</a> by the demon king Ravana and her rescue; retold in countless versions, among them the Ramcharitmanas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulsidas">Tulsidas</a> in the 16th century, it is performed across South and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>.</p><p>The law books, of which the Manusmriti is the most famous, set out the duties of the classes and stages of life. The Puranas, compiled from the Gupta period onwards, gathered myths of creation, genealogies of gods and kings, and instructions for worship and pilgrimage.</p><p>Great teachers shaped the tradition. Shankara, in the 8th century, taught the non-dual Advaita <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanuja">Ramanuja</a>, in the 11th and 12th centuries, taught a qualified non-dualism that made room for devotion to a personal God; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhvacharya">Madhva</a>, in the 13th, taught a dualism of God and souls. The Tamil poet-saints, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvars">Alvars</a> devoted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayanars">Nayanars</a> devoted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, composed hymns from the 6th to the 9th centuries that launched the bhakti movement, which later spread north through poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirabai">Mirabai</a> and Tulsidas. In modern times Ramakrishna, Vivekananda and Mohandas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> gave Hindu ideas new forms and a world audience.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/angkor-wat.jpg" alt="Angkor Wat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Angkor_Wat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bjørn Christian Tørrissen, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hinduism grew across the subcontinent as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> culture spread from the north into the south, east and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a>, carried by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, ascetics, merchants and kings who sponsored temples and rites. Royal patronage made it the religion of most Indian kingdoms, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Guptas</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> of the south, whose great temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> was completed around 1010.</p><p>Through trade and the courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, Hindu gods, epics and ideas of kingship travelled across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>. From the early centuries CE, kingdoms in Funan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> lands adopted Sanskrit, Hindu rites and the worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, often alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a>, built in the early 12th century for the Khmer king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suryavarman-ii">Suryavarman II</a>, was dedicated to Vishnu, and the temples of Prambanan in Java to Shiva in the 9th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> later became the main religion of Java, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> has remained Hindu to this day, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> is still performed in dance and puppet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> across the region.</p><p>From the end of the 12th century Muslim dynasties ruled much of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, first the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> and from the 16th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. Hinduism remained the religion of most of the population, and the long encounter produced conflict and destruction of temples but also exchange: the bhakti poets and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufis</a> shared ideas, and figures such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabir">Kabir</a> drew on both traditions.</p><p>In the 19th century, under British rule, reformers responded to Christian missionaries and Western criticism. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-mohan-roy">Ram Mohan Roy</a> founded the Brahmo Samaj in 1828 to promote a rational, monotheistic Hinduism; Dayananda <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati">Saraswati</a> founded the Arya Samaj in 1875 to return to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swami-vivekananda">Swami Vivekananda</a>’s address to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worlds-parliament-of-religions">World’s Parliament of Religions</a> in Chicago in 1893 introduced Hindu philosophy to Western audiences.</p><p>Indentured labourers taken from India by the British in the 19th century founded Hindu communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>, Guyana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, and migration in the 20th century carried Hinduism to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> and the Gulf.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mirabai"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mirabai.jpg" alt="Mirabai" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mirabai">Mirabai</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kangra_painting_of_Mirabai,_the_female_Bhakti_saint.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hindu society has been organised for much of its history by the system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a> and jati. The law books describe four varnas, or classes: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, priests and teachers; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriyas</a>, rulers and warriors; Vaishyas, merchants and farmers; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudras</a>, labourers and servants. In practice society was divided into thousands of jatis, hereditary groups linked to occupation and region that governed marriage, diet and social contact. Below them were communities treated as “untouchable”, subjected to severe discrimination. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">Caste</a> has been criticised from within Hinduism, by bhakti poets who taught that devotion makes all equal and by modern reformers including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a>, and the constitution of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> abolished untouchability in 1950, though caste remains a force in social and political life.</p><p>Temples have long been centres of community life and wealth. Medieval South Indian temples owned land, employed priests, musicians, dancers and craftsmen, lent money and fed pilgrims. Monasteries founded by Shankara and later teachers, and the orders of ascetics, the sadhus and sannyasins, preserved learning and teaching.</p><p>Much Hindu religious life takes place in the home: daily worship at the household shrine, the rites of the family’s life cycle, vegetarian diet in many communities, and the observance of fasts and festivals. Women have often been the keepers of household rites and devotional song, and some of the greatest bhakti poets, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirabai">Mirabai</a> and the Tamil saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andal">Andal</a>, were women; women also faced restrictions in the classical law books, and practices such as the burning of widows, sati, were opposed by reformers and banned under British rule in 1829.</p><p>Hinduism has no central authority. Teachers, gurus, temple traditions and regional customs carry it. In the 20th and 21st centuries it has also become a political identity: Hindu nationalism, which seeks to define India as a Hindu nation, has been a major force in Indian politics, while India’s constitution establishes a secular state.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/madurai"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/madurai.jpg" alt="Madurai" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meenakshi_Amman_West_Tower.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KennyOMG, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hinduism is the world’s third-largest religion, with about 1.2 billion followers, the great majority of them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where it is the faith of about four in five people, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. It is also one of the oldest living religious traditions, with scriptures in continuous recitation for three thousand years.</p><p>Its influence reaches far beyond its followers. The religions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhism</a> grew in dialogue with it and share many of its ideas, such as karma and rebirth. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> literature it produced, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> and the epics to drama and poetry, is one of the great literatures of the world, and its stories are told in the arts of all of South and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>.</p><p>Hindu philosophy, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a>, influenced thinkers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and America from the 19th century onwards, among them Arthur Schopenhauer and the American Transcendentalists. Practices of yoga and meditation, rooted in Hindu traditions and the Yoga Sutras of Patanjali, have spread worldwide in adapted forms, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>’s non-violent resistance, inspired in part by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>, shaped movements for civil rights across the world.</p><p>Hindu temple architecture produced some of the great monuments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, from the temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khajuraho">Khajuraho</a> and the Meenakshi temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a> and Prambanan. Classical music and dance traditions such as Bharatanatyam grew from temple performance. Its festivals, Diwali above all, are now celebrated in cities around the world.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Hinduism, which grew from the Vedic religion of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and absorbed many local traditions, has no founder or single creed but shares the ideas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a>, karma, rebirth and release and devotion to gods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and the Goddess. Its scriptures, temples and philosophies shaped South and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, and today it is the world’s third-largest religion.</em></p>

